Q: Unable to assign values to pointer without scanf() A: <p>Your code is so poorly written that I had no idea where to start, so I just rewrote the entire thing.</p>
<p>Before copying the code directly, please read it carefully.</p>
<p>The issues are:</p>
<ol>
<li><code>strcmp</code> cannot be used with <code>char</code> (single-quoted characters), only with <code>string</code> (double-quoted strings).</li>
<li>Use the less common <code>switch-case</code> instead of <code>if</code> statements.</li>
<li>There no functions like <code>mem.len()</code> in C. Consider passing all data via a structure or using input-accepting pointers (like <code>scanf</code>). Note: This requires counting ‘o’ characters again in <code>main()</code>.</li>
<li>When asking questions, fully describe your requirements. It took me considerable effort to deduce from your code that you were building a DSL interpreter.</li>
</ol>
<p>Here is the code:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;stddef.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

typedef struct {
    int *data;
    int len;
} IntArray;

IntArray parse(const char *program)
{
    IntArray result = { NULL, 0 };

    int num = 0;

    // count o
    for (int i = 0; program[i] != '\0'; i++) {
        if (program[i] == 'o') {
            result.len++;
        }
    }

    if (result.len == 0) {
        return result;  // data = NULL, len = 0
    }

    result.data = malloc(sizeof(int) * result.len);
    if (!result.data) {
        result.len = 0;
        return result;
    }

    // Exec code
    int k = 0;
    for (int i = 0; program[i] != '\0'; i++) {
        switch (program[i]) {
            case 'i':
                num++;
                break;
            case 'd':
                num--;
                break;
            case 's':
                num *= num;
                break;
            case 'o':
                result.data[k++] = num;
                break;
        }
    }

    return result;
}

int main(void)
{
    const char *input = &quot;ioioio&quot;;

    IntArray output = parse(input);

    for (int i = 0; i &lt; output.len; i++) {
        printf(&quot;%d &quot;, output.data[i]);
    }
    printf(&quot;\n&quot;);
    // (base) kagari@ArchLinux:~$ gcc test.c -o test;./test
    // 1 2 3 

    free(output.data);
    return 0;
}


</code></pre>
